WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.870
Film Courage: Passive versus active main character?

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:03.760
Paul Joseph Gulino, Associate Professor at
Chapman University Dodge College of Film and

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.690
Media Arts: Okay a passive person.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:11.150
This is an opportunity situation because most
people assume and most movies are about somebody

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.119
who is active and they’ve got to do something.

00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:21.160
But there are successful pictures where you
have someone who is not and how does that

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:26.830
work that’s what you look for everybody
says do this and this one doesn’t and it

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.090
still works, so why?

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.630
What did they do that compensated for it.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:34.290
Because obviously if you have an active main
character it’s easy to watch.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:38.210
You are going to see if they get what they
want or not and it’s all taken care of.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.370
But what about that?

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:43.840
What you’ll see is there has to be conflict.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:51.550
As long as there is conflict you will be okay
because the conflict will create the suspense

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:57.950
and the tension that evades us if you are
using dramatic tension as the main tool.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:59.300
Not all movies do.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:04.129
There will be ensemble pieces that will be
following a bunch of different stories and

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:07.680
different characters and the unifying element
is the milieu like DINER.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.731
It’s about a diner, it’s what happens
in a diner.

00:01:09.731 --> 00:01:15.090
And as long as it’s understood by the audience
that that is what they are watching it’s

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:16.450
a signal about what it’s about.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.500
In fact that movie is a 1982 picture.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:26.340
I believe the opening title is The Week Before
Christmas 1959 (something like that).

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.600
Maybe 1958?

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.590
It’s about cults or something.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:34.409
But it gives you a sense right away from the
opening that it’s going to take place in

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:35.460
about a week.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.250
It actually starts Christmas Eve and it actually
takes place in a week.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:47.220
So that frames it and then you have an ensemble
and there are competitive conflicts with those

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:54.250
but the overall conflict doesn’t utilize
dramatic conflict as bait so it doesn’t

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:55.250
have that.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:02:01.090
You have first acts involving different characters
and different subplots and a subplot has its

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.170
own act structure (generally).

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:09.710
There are a couple that kind of dangle off
and disappear (the main ones do).

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:14.161
But if we go back to the most common form
which is a character with an objective and

00:02:14.161 --> 00:02:18.060
there is obstacles an active one helps.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.880
There was a famous editor I was told about
and I don’t know who it was but that the

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.400
secret to the craft is that all scenes are
either chases or escapes.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.840
People are either trying to get something
or trying to avoid something.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.700
And a passive character is just somebody who
is trying to avoid something and the key is

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.680
to build a circumstance where that is hard.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:45.890
Like LARS AND THE REAL GIRL, we talked about
that.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:50.580
This is a movie where we have a passive main
character where all he wants to do is…it’s

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.180
an escape story because the whole town has
united to not let him do what he wants to

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.760
do by very indirect means playing along.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:03.580
But you can see the tension is there and surrounds
the question is he ever going to get better?

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.290
So that would be the passive main character.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.060
Or I like [ONE FLEW OVER THE] CUCKOO’S NEST.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.340
What does this guy want to do, he wants to
avoid doing any work.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.420
He wants to play cards!

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.739
He doesn’t have any agenda beyond that.

00:03:14.739 --> 00:03:20.900
But the way the story is constructed it’s
the worst possible situation for his unique

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:30.360
characteristics which is that he is a noble
person and he wants to help people and this

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.000
world crushes him.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.040
So that’s something you can look for.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.319
Film Courage: THE GRADUATE…sorry.

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:44.959
Paul: THE GRADUATE, he doesn’t have to pursue
her but I do have an analysis in the book.

00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:47.830
He doesn’t have to pursue her, she is in
his lap.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:49.670
There is nothing there.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:56.090
But what keeps us suspended there is that
it’s not an escape movie, it’s dramatic

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:57.450
irony.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:02.380
You look at the middle part of that movie
example where the second act is actually not

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.950
that long, it’s about equal in size with
the first and third act.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:11.400
The second act is really about whether he
will get caught and you have comic irony played

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.180
with (and sometimes dramatic irony) where
the mother inquires “Where do you go every

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:17.180
night?”

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:18.229
He cuts himself while shaving.

00:04:18.229 --> 00:04:21.400
You can see he is not comfortable answering
“Oh I just drive.”

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:29.779
Or in the hotel where everybody recognizes
him and he is with the girl and Catherine

00:04:29.779 --> 00:04:37.530
Ross the actress at the hotel where he’s
been having his liaisons and everybody knows

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:46.770
him and he’s trying to conceal from her
what was happening.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:54.689
SUNSET BOULEVARD is a movie about this guy
who is trying to hustle this older woman (he’s

00:04:54.689 --> 00:05:02.930
a writer) and after a certain point, really
from the middle of the movie on, he’s really

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:03.930
passive.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.610
He’s kind of avoiding the world.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:12.219
But it’s an escape in that way, he’s trying
to stay away from everything and the world

00:05:12.219 --> 00:05:13.219
intrudes.

00:05:13.219 --> 00:05:19.180
But largely that one is sustain by a little
bit of tension about somebody trying to not

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:28.349
let him escape and a voiceover narration which
keeps us…it is a form of telegraphing “This

00:05:28.349 --> 00:05:29.490
is about to happen.”

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:38.039
Or little did I know this would happen so
that we are anticipating.

00:05:38.039 --> 00:05:41.149
So the answer to the question can you have
a passive main character look for where the

00:05:41.149 --> 00:05:42.430
conflicts are.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:46.659
If the character is passive and never wants
to do anything, can you put them in a situation

00:05:46.659 --> 00:05:51.599
that challenges that and makes them fight?

